            <ArticleTitle>Arg16 ADRB2 genotype increases the risk of asthma exacerbation in children with a reported use of long-acting β2-agonists: results of the PACMAN cohort.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1965-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/pgs.13.200</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Current evidence suggests that asthma patients with the ADRB2 Arg16 genotype have a poorer response to long-acting β2-agonists (LABA), but the results remain inconsistent.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study assessed the association between Arg16 variants and treatment outcome in children treated with inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) and LABA.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS &amp; METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">ADRB2 Arg16 was genotyped in 597 children (4-12 years of age) participating in the PACMAN cohort study. A questionnaire was used to assess asthma control, frequency of asthma-related emergency department visits and use of oral corticosteroids in the past year.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Arg/Arg carriers with a reported use of ICS and LABA had an increased risk of oral corticosteroid use (odds ratio: 14.9; 95% CI: 1.59-140.1) and emergency department visits in the past year (odds ratio: 11.9; 95% CI: 1.22-115.8) compared to Gly/Gly carriers. This effect was not observed in Arg/Arg genotype carriers reporting ICS use only.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Children who are homozygous for ADRB2 Arg16 have an increased risk of exacerbations when treated with combined LABA and ICS.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
